WEBVTT
TITLE Secondary Storage and RAID: Disk Geometry, Access Time and the Five Levels — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A memory hierarchy is a set of memories that trade latency against cost per byte.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
One sector read from this disk takes 12.18 milliseconds; a DRAM read takes 60 nanoseconds.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.500
The disk is 100 times cheaper per byte, so it holds what will not fit — and it must be read in blocks.

4
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:27.000
Two consequences follow: an access is mostly waiting, and the wait is decided by position.

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.500
A platter has two surfaces; each surface has concentric tracks; each track is cut into sectors.

6
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:42.000
Capacity is the product: 8 surfaces times 1000 cylinders times 500 sectors times 512 bytes.

7
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.500
That is 1.9073 GiB, not 2.048 GB — and a drive sold as 1 TB shows 931 GiB for the same reason.

8
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:57.000
One track is 256,000 bytes and one cylinder is 2,048,000 bytes — reachable with a single seek.

9
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000
A logical block address is a single number; the disk needs three.

10
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Divide by sectors-per-cylinder, then by sectors-per-track: 4,000 and 500 here.

11
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Address 3,999,999 is the last sector: cylinder 999, surface 7, sector 500.

12
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Two addresses in the same cylinder differ by no seek — only by a surface switch.

13
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.500
An access is three waits in a row: seek, then rotation, then transfer.

14
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:36.000
Rotation is 60,000 over 7,200 milliseconds per turn: 8.3333 ms, so the average wait is 4.1667 ms.

15
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:43.500
Transfer of one 512-byte sector is 8.3333 divided by 500: 0.0167 ms, and seek is given as 8 ms.

16
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:51.000
Seek is 66 per cent of the total and rotation 34 per cent; the transfer is one seventh of one per cent.

17
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.000
The head sits still and the platter turns: the target sector arrives at some phase between 0 and one turn.

18
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Averaging the phase over a whole turn gives exactly half: the area under a ramp from 0 to 8.3333.

19
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.000
Best case 0, average 4.1667, worst 8.3333 ms — and the worst is twice the average.

20
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Reading k sectors adds k sector-times, because the head is already in place.

21
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
One sector: 12.1833 ms for 512 bytes, which is 42 kB/s.

22
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Eight sectors, one 4 KB block: 12.3 ms and 333 kB/s. The extra seven sectors cost 0.1167 ms.

23
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Sixty-four sectors: 13.2333 ms, 2.48 MB/s. A whole track: 20.5 ms, 12.49 MB/s.

24
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Full track against one sector is 297 times the throughput, on the same disk, with the same head.

25
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:51.000
RAID 0 cuts the data into blocks and deals them round the disks, one block each.

26
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:57.000
Six disks of 1 TB give 6 TB usable — 100 per cent — and six times the read bandwidth.

27
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.000
One disk fails and the stripe set is gone: the surviving blocks are not a file, they are a fragment.

28
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.000
A hundred disks at 200,000 hours each give an array MTTF of 2,000 hours — about three months.

29
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:15.000
RAID 1 writes every block twice, on two disks. A read may be served by either.

30
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Six disks of 1 TB give 3 TB usable: 50 per cent. Every write costs two disk operations.

31
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.000
Any one failure is survivable. Of the fifteen pairs of failures, three are fatal: both disks of one pair.

32
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Mirroring is the same as RAID 5 with a parity group of two, and the capacity tells you so.

33
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Parity is one extra block per stripe, equal to the exclusive-or of the data blocks.

34
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Lose any one block and exclusive-or the rest with the parity: the missing byte comes back exactly.

35
00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:00.000
A small write is four operations: read old data, read old parity, write new data, write new parity.

36
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:09.000
RAID 4 sends every parity write to one disk; RAID 5 rotates it, so writes go in parallel.

37
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.750
RAID 6 adds a second check block, Q, computed with weights over the field of 256 bytes.

38
00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:22.500
Two lost blocks are solved from two equations: the model recovers all fifteen pairs exactly.

39
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:29.250
Six disks give 4 TB usable, 66.67 per cent, and a small write costs six operations.

40
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:36.000
RAID 6 survives every pattern of up to two failures: 22 of the 64 patterns, and 0.0000196 at p = 0.01.

41
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:43.500
Four numbers decide the level: usable capacity, fault tolerance, write penalty, and the chance of loss.

42
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:51.000
RAID 5 is the cheapest redundancy; RAID 6 buys a second failure for one more disk of cost.

43
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:58.500
RAID 10 spends half the capacity on copies and keeps the write penalty at two.

44
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:06.000
The rule: capacity decides where the data fits, the write penalty decides the workload, redundancy decides the risk.

